<div class="<%= layout_framework_classes %>">
  <div class="<%= layout_framework_title_classes %>">
    <%= render 'framework/page_header', page_name: :color_palettes %>
  </div>

  <div class="<%= layout_framework_content_classes %>">
    <%= render_framework_intro_paragraph(:color_palettes) %>

    <div class="space-y-16">
      <section class="space-y-8">
        <div class="space-y-4">
          <%= framework_heading(3, 'Grayscale palettes') %>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            Each of the four grayscale palettes maps onto a bit-depth class on the
            <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :screen) %> element, so a grayscale screen carries
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">screen--1bit</code>,
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">screen--2bit</code>, or
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">screen--4bit</code> instead of a palette class.
          </p>
          <ul class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %> list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
            <li><code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">bw</code> (2 levels) renders as <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">screen--1bit</code>. Every color renders as a black and white dither pattern.</li>
            <li><code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">gray-4</code> (4 grays) renders as <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">screen--2bit</code>.</li>
            <li><code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">gray-16</code> (16 grays) renders as <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">screen--4bit</code>, where the grays paint as solids.</li>
            <li><code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">gray-256</code> renders as <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">screen--4bit</code> too. It is a delivery format, not a fifth tier.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="space-y-4">
          <%= framework_heading(4, 'Two ways to deliver 16 levels') %>
          <p class="<%= framework_subsection_description_classes %>">
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">gray-16</code> and
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">gray-256</code> share a class because they share the
            glass: 16 panel levels either way. They differ in who reduces the image.
          </p>
          <ul class="<%= framework_subsection_description_classes %> list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
            <li><code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">gray-16</code>: the platform posterizes and dithers to those 16 levels before it sends the screen.</li>
            <li><code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">gray-256</code>: the platform sends a smooth 8-bit PNG and the device quantizes it to its own 16 levels.</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="<%= framework_subsection_description_classes %>">
            The registry name for <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">gray-256</code>, Smooth
            Grayscale (device-quantized), describes that delivery, not 256 displayable grays. Build against
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">screen--4bit</code>.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-8">
        <div class="space-y-4">
          <%= framework_heading(3, 'Color palette classes') %>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            Every color panel the framework supports has a palette class on the
            <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :screen) %> element. Five classes cover the limited-ink e-paper panels;
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">screen--color-full</code> covers displays that render
            arbitrary color.
          </p>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            A device can support more than one palette. Inky Impression 7.3 carries six: black and white, 7-color,
            6-color, 4-ink B/W/R/Y, 3-ink B/W/R, and 3-ink B/W/Y. The selected mode picks the class.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="<%= framework_docs_table_wrapper_classes %>">
          <div class="<%= framework_docs_table_card_classes %>">
            <table class="<%= framework_docs_table_classes %>">
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th class="<%= framework_docs_table_header_cell_classes %>">Class</th>
                  <th class="<%= framework_docs_table_header_cell_classes %>">Inks</th>
                  <th class="<%= framework_docs_table_header_cell_classes %>">Panels</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody class="<%= framework_docs_table_body_classes %>">
                <tr>
                  <td class="px-6 py-4 whitespace-nowrap text-sm"><code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">screen--color-3bwr</code></td>
                  <td class="px-6 py-4 text-sm">Black, white, red</td>
                  <td class="px-6 py-4 text-sm text-gray-500 dark:text-gray-400">Waveshare 7.5" B/W/R</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td class="px-6 py-4 whitespace-nowrap text-sm"><code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">screen--color-3bwy</code></td>
                  <td class="px-6 py-4 text-sm">Black, white, yellow</td>
                  <td class="px-6 py-4 text-sm text-gray-500 dark:text-gray-400">No native B/W/Y panel yet, but nine profiles offer it as a mode (Inky Impression 7.3 and 13.3, Seeed E1002 and E1004, and others)</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td class="px-6 py-4 whitespace-nowrap text-sm"><code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">screen--color-4bwry</code></td>
                  <td class="px-6 py-4 text-sm">Black, white, red, yellow</td>
                  <td class="px-6 py-4 text-sm text-gray-500 dark:text-gray-400">TRMNL OG (B/W/R/Y), Waveshare 7.5" B/W/R/Y</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td class="px-6 py-4 whitespace-nowrap text-sm"><code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">screen--color-6a</code></td>
                  <td class="px-6 py-4 text-sm">Black, white, red, green, blue, yellow</td>
                  <td class="px-6 py-4 text-sm text-gray-500 dark:text-gray-400">Inky Impression 7.3 and 13.3, Seeed E1002 and E1004</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td class="px-6 py-4 whitespace-nowrap text-sm"><code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">screen--color-7a</code></td>
                  <td class="px-6 py-4 text-sm">Black, white, red, green, blue, yellow, orange</td>
                  <td class="px-6 py-4 text-sm text-gray-500 dark:text-gray-400">Inkplate 6COLOR, Inky Impression 7.3</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td class="px-6 py-4 whitespace-nowrap text-sm"><code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">screen--color-full</code></td>
                  <td class="px-6 py-4 text-sm">Whatever the display can show</td>
                  <td class="px-6 py-4 text-sm text-gray-500 dark:text-gray-400">12-bit and 24-bit displays: Onyx BOOX Nova Air C, Tidbyt</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-8">
        <div class="space-y-4">
          <%= framework_heading(3, 'Full color') %>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            Two palettes share <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">screen--color-full</code>.
          </p>
          <ul class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %> list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
            <li><code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">color-24bit</code> (16.7 million colors) covers LCD, OLED, browser, and virtual screens. The render ships as lossless sRGB: no remap, no dithering, no posterize.</li>
            <li><code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">color-12bit</code> (4096 colors) covers Kaleido-class color e-paper. It shares the class, and the platform posterizes the render to those 4096 colors.</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            Both report <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">--framework-bit-depth: 12</code>, the lower
            of the two, so code reading the depth never over-promises what a Kaleido panel prints. Every color the
            framework paints is sRGB.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="mt-4">
          <%= render CodeExampleComponent.new(
            demo_code: <<~'HTML'.strip
              <div class="screen screen--generic_16_9 screen--color-full">
                <div class="view view--full">
                  <!-- bg--red-60 is a solid red here, a dither on every limited palette -->
                  <div class="layout bg--red-60">
                    <span class="title">Delayed</span>
                  </div>
                </div>
              </div>
            HTML
          ) %>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-8">
        <div class="space-y-4">
          <%= framework_heading(3, 'Every palette and its class') %>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            Every palette maps to one framework class. That class also reports its paint depth as
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">--framework-bit-depth</code>, which
            <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :paint_api) %> reads.
          </p>
        </div>

        <%# Depths mirror $_screen-paint-depths in base/_screen-mode-vars.scss, where the registry lives. %>
        <% paint_depths = { 'screen--1bit' => 1, 'screen--2bit' => 2, 'screen--4bit' => 4, 'screen--color-full' => 12 } %>
        <% limited_palette_depth = 4 %>

        <div class="<%= framework_docs_table_wrapper_classes %>">
          <div class="<%= framework_docs_table_card_classes %>">
            <table class="<%= framework_docs_table_classes %>">
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th class="<%= framework_docs_table_header_cell_classes %>">Palette</th>
                  <th class="<%= framework_docs_table_header_cell_classes %>">Registry name</th>
                  <th class="<%= framework_docs_table_header_cell_classes %>">Framework class</th>
                  <th class="<%= framework_docs_table_header_cell_classes %>">Published depth</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody class="<%= framework_docs_table_body_classes %>">
                <% framework_palette_options.each do |palette| %>
                  <tr>
                    <td class="px-6 py-4 whitespace-nowrap text-sm"><code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>"><%= palette[:id] %></code></td>
                    <td class="px-6 py-4 text-sm"><%= palette[:name] %></td>
                    <td class="px-6 py-4 whitespace-nowrap text-sm"><code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>"><%= palette[:framework_class] %></code></td>
                    <td class="px-6 py-4 text-sm text-gray-500 dark:text-gray-400"><%= paint_depths.fetch(palette[:framework_class], limited_palette_depth) %></td>
                  </tr>
                <% end %>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        </div>

        <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
          The depth describes how the screen paints, not the panel's raw capability. Limited palettes print solid
          inks the way 4-bit prints solid grays, so they report 4.
        </p>

        <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
          <strong>The numeric variants stop at <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">4bit:</code>.</strong>
          Grayscale glass stops at 16 levels, so
          <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">1bit:</code>,
          <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">2bit:</code>, and
          <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">4bit:</code> cover every grayscale palette. Color has
          its own classes and no numeric prefix; see
          <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :rendering_modes) %>.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-8">
        <div class="space-y-4">
          <%= framework_heading(3, 'How Colors Print per Palette') %>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            A limited palette prints solid inks and nothing in between, so the framework dithers every color in the
            <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :colors) %> palette down to the panel's inks. Your markup does not
            change: the same <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">bg--</code> and
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">text--</code> utilities print differently per palette.
          </p>
          <ul class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %> list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
            <li><strong>Grays dither in black and white.</strong> On every limited palette, <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">bg--gray-50</code> is a 1-bit pattern rather than a solid gray.</li>
            <li><strong>Hues snap to the closest ink.</strong> On <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">screen--color-3bwr</code> every hue prints red, so blue and green stop reading as separate categories. <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">screen--color-7a</code> carries its own orange ink.</li>
            <li><strong>Shade steps dither the ink.</strong> <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">bg--red-20</code> mixes red with black and <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">bg--red-60</code> mixes red with white, which is how one ink covers a ladder of shades.</li>
            <li><strong>Full color skips dithering.</strong> <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">screen--color-full</code> paints every token as a solid color.</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            <strong>Device Preview tip:</strong> Pick a color device in the Device Preview (top right) to see the
            palette applied to every demo on this site.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-8">
        <div class="space-y-4">
          <%= framework_heading(3, 'Applying a palette') %>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            The palette class sits on the screen, next to the device class; grayscale palettes put their bit-depth
            class in the same place. Who writes it depends on where the screen renders.
          </p>
        </div>

        <%= render FrameworkComparisonComponent.new(variant: :platform_vs_custom) do |c| %>
          <% c.with_left_message do %>
            <p class="text-sm font-medium">You don't write the palette class. The platform renders your layout against the device's own profile, so one plugin covers 1-bit panels and 7-color panels alike.</p>
          <% end %>
          <% c.with_right_message do %>
            <p class="text-sm font-medium">You add the palette class to the screen yourself, next to the device class, and the framework repaints every token for that ink set.</p>
          <% end %>
          <% c.with_left_code do %>
            <%= render CodeExampleComponent.new(
              demo_code: <<~'HTML'.strip,
                <!-- Your layout; the platform supplies the screen and its classes -->
                <div class="layout">
                  <span class="title">Northbound</span>
                  <span class="label label--primary">On time</span>
                </div>
              HTML
              extra_classes: '!rounded-t-none'
            ) %>
          <% end %>
          <% c.with_right_code do %>
            <%= render CodeExampleComponent.new(
              demo_code: <<~'HTML'.strip,
                <div class="screen screen--inky_impression_7_3 screen--color-7a">
                  <div class="view view--full">
                    <div class="layout">
                      <span class="title">Northbound</span>
                      <span class="label label--primary">On time</span>
                    </div>
                  </div>
                </div>
              HTML
              extra_classes: '!rounded-t-none'
            ) %>
          <% end %>
        <% end %>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-8">
        <div class="space-y-4">
          <%= framework_heading(3, 'Preview modes') %>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            Panel inks are darker and flatter than the same hex on a monitor. Two modifiers repaint a limited palette
            with device-accurate values so a preview on a screen matches the print.
          </p>
          <ul class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %> list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
            <li><code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">screen--preview-colors</code>: swaps the full-bright inks for the muted ones the panel actually prints.</li>
            <li><code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">screen--preview-white-limited</code>: mutes white to the panel's off-white. Add it alongside <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">screen--preview-colors</code> for panels whose white is not paper white.</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            The Device Preview's Raw Colors and Preview Colors toggle sets both for you. Rendered output on a device
            uses neither.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="mt-4">
          <%= render CodeExampleComponent.new(
            demo_code: <<~'HTML'.strip
              <!-- Device-accurate preview of a 6-color panel -->
              <div class="screen screen--color-6a screen--preview-colors screen--preview-white-limited">
                ...
              </div>
            HTML
          ) %>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-8">
        <div class="space-y-4">
          <%= framework_heading(3, 'Targeting a palette in SCSS') %>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            Scope your own SCSS to a palette with the same mixins the framework uses. The mixin ids drop the
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">color-</code> prefix the class carries:
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">'3bwr'</code>,
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">'3bwy'</code>,
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">'4bwry'</code>,
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">'6a'</code>, and
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">'7a'</code>.
          </p>
          <ul class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %> list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
            <li><code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">for-color-palette($id)</code>: scopes to one limited palette.</li>
            <li><code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">for-color-full</code>: scopes to full-color displays.</li>
            <li><code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">for-preview-color-palette($id)</code>: scopes to the device-accurate preview of one limited palette.</li>
            <li><code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">for-1bit</code>, <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">for-2bit</code>, <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">for-4bit</code>: scope to a grayscale tier.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="mt-4">
          <%= render CodeExampleComponent.new(demo_code: '@use \'framework/mixins\' as trmnl;

.legend-swatch {
    width: 12px;
    height: 12px;

    // Three-ink panels print every hue in the same accent, so carry the
    // distinction in shape instead of color.
    @include trmnl.for-color-palette(\'3bwr\') {
        border-radius: 50%;
    }

    @include trmnl.for-color-full {
        border-radius: 0;
    }
}', language: 'scss') %>
        </div>

        <div class="space-y-4">
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            The full mixin surface is documented on <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :sass_mixins) %>, and
            <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :sass_devices) %> covers adding a device profile that selects a palette.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-8">
        <div class="space-y-4">
          <%= framework_heading(3, 'Palettes from JavaScript') %>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            TRMNLPaint returns what CSS would paint on the current palette, so JavaScript needs no palette handling
            of its own. Ask on a 7-color screen and the answer carries that palette's dither; the same call on a
            full-color screen returns a solid. See <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :paint_api) %>.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <%= render 'framework/page_navigation' %>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
